From: octopus <octopus@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] wayland: don't let a stale touch point take a new contact's up

touch_handle_up() releases the first *pressed* point with the contact's
id, whatever surface it is on. A point whose up never arrives (its
surface was destroyed mid-touch, e.g. a popup window closed under the
finger) stays pressed in the seat's list forever; on single-touch
hardware every later contact has the same id, so each later up releases
that dead point instead, and the new point stays pressed. The window it
is on then reads "pressed" for good and ignores all taps.

A down for an id means any earlier contact with that id is over, so
drop pressed points with that id before adding the new one.

--- a/src/drivers/wayland/lv_wayland_touch.c
+++ b/src/drivers/wayland/lv_wayland_touch.c
@@ -208,6 +208,15 @@
     tdata->touches[i].state     = LV_INDEV_STATE_PRESSED;
     tdata->event_cnt++;
 #else
+    /* A down reuses the id of a contact that is over: drop any point still
+     * pressed with it (its up never came, e.g. its surface was destroyed
+     * mid-touch). Left in the list, it would take this contact's up, and
+     * this point would stay pressed forever. */
+    lv_wl_touch_point_t * stale;
+    while((stale = get_pressed_touch_point(tdata, id)) != NULL) {
+        touch_point_delete(tdata, stale);
+    }
+
     lv_wl_touch_point_t * touch_point = lv_ll_ins_tail(&tdata->touch_point_ll);
     LV_ASSERT_MALLOC(touch_point);
     if(!touch_point) {
